If Statement:
If statements allow us to execute code with conditions.
To use an if statement in Java, we need to use curly braces {} to define the code block, and everything inside the if statemen
should be placed between these braces:
Syantax-
     if (condition) {
         code;
         code;
         code;
      }
Example-
    int age = 20;
    String status = "Child";
    if (age > 18) {
        status = "Adult";
     }
    age += 1;  //In the end, the code will increment age by 1 whether the age is bigger than 18 or not.

IF-ELSE STATEMENT:
if allows us to execute particular code if a condition is met, but what if we want to execute something else if the condition is not met?
For that we have the else statement:
     int age = 15;
     String status = "None";
     if (age >= 18) {
         status = "Adult";
     } else {
         status = "Young";
     }
In the above example, age is smaller than 18 which means it enters the else code, and status will hold "Young".
We can even make it more profound using the else if statement:
     int age = 68;
     String status = "None";
     if (age < 18) {
         status = "Young";
     } else if (age >= 18 && age <= 65) {
         status = "Adult";
     } else {
         status = "Old";
     }

SWITCH STATEMENT:
The switch statement is like a multi-way if statement. Instead of evaluating a single condition, it checks the value of a variable against multiple cases and 
executes the code associated with the matching case.
Here's the basic structure of a switch statement:
      switch (variable) {
            case value1:
                     // Code to execute if variable equals value1
             break;
            case value2:
                     // Code to execute if variable equals value2
             break;
                    // ... more cases
             default:
                    // Code to execute if no case matches
     }

1.The switch keyword is followed by the variable you want to test in parentheses.
2.Each case represents a possible value of the variable.
3.The code inside each case is executed if the variable matches that case's value.
4.The break statement is crucial; it exits the switch after a case is executed. Without it, execution would "fall through" to the next case.
5.The default case is optional and is executed if no other case matches.

TERNARY OPERATOR:
The ternary operator is a simple one-line if-else statement. It has the following syntax:
     variable = (condition) ? value_if_true : value_if_false;
For example;
     int age = 20;
     String message = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
